    {
      "id": "c8f15983-0462-4def-9c6f-194784901f03",
      "type": "content",
      "label": "Chat GPT Comments",
      "root": false,
      "position": {
        "x": 545.8879498485462,
        "y": -1121.9922327328486
      },
      "width": 793.526089551116,
      "height": 514.578817401705,
      "ports": [
        {
          "id": "root",
          "label": "root",
          "direction": "input",
          "dataType": "any",
          "angle": 270,
          "portId": "root",
          "metadata": {
            "interface": true,
            "structural": true,
            "role": "view.content"
          }
        }
      ],
      "visible": true,
      "showLabel": true,
      "data": {
        "content": {
          "kind": "markdown",
          "value": "I tested the actual graph. **The core model is good, but I don't think Methods is quite as complete as Properties was.** The graph correctly defines a Method as a first-class callable operation with declared inputs, outputs, effects, authority, and an implementation that may live separately. It also explicitly keeps implementation language, sandboxing, async behavior, and retries open. \n\nThe biggest thing I think is missing is **invocation identity/context**.\n\nRight now you essentially have:\n\n`arguments → authority check → implementation → result/error`\n\nBut when a method runs, I'd want the runtime to know something conceptually like:\n\n`method + owner + caller + arguments + invocation`\n\nThat **invocation** should probably be first-class, even if it isn't necessarily persisted as a graph node. It gives you somewhere to hang status, timing, progress, cancellation, errors, result, tracing, and eventually events. Without it, asynchronous methods get awkward very quickly.\n\nThe second thing I'd tighten is **effects**. Your Method definition already says methods declare effects, which I really like.  But I'd explicitly distinguish:\n\n**inputs/outputs describe data flow; effects describe mutations or interactions with the world.**\n\nSo a method might declare:\n\n`inputs: [amount]`\n`output: invoice`\n`effects: [create node, write property, external request]`\n\nThat becomes enormously valuable for agents and authority. Twilite can potentially inspect what a method *may do* before allowing it to execute. 🔐\n\nThe third missing concept is **sync versus async result semantics**, without committing to an execution engine. I'd define an invocation as producing an **outcome**, where the outcome can be immediate or pending. Then you don't need two kinds of methods. A method invocation simply has a lifecycle:\n\n`requested → authorized → running → succeeded | failed | cancelled`\n\nProgress can exist while running. That dovetails beautifully with Events later.\n\nAnd there's one distinction from Properties that I think you should preserve very deliberately:\n\n> **Calling a method is not the same thing as its effects occurring.**\n\nThat's the Method equivalent of our Property rule:\n\n> **A write is an action; a value change is an outcome.**\n\nSo:\n\n**An invocation is an action; effects and results are outcomes.**\n\nA method can be invoked and denied. Invoked and fail. Invoked and succeed without mutation. Invoked and cause five mutations. That's going to keep your event semantics wonderfully clean.\n\nThere's also a small terminology question I'd settle now: **owner vs receiver**. Your graph says a method is attached to an \"owner or graph.\"  I'd distinguish the thing that *declares/provides* the method from the thing the invocation is *operating on*. They may usually be the same initially, but inheritance/templates/classes could eventually make them different.\n\nSo I'd add four concepts before calling this one cooked:\n\n**Invocation**: a particular attempt to execute a Method.\n\n**Invocation context**: caller/actor, receiver, arguments, authority context, perhaps graph/workspace context.\n\n**Outcome**: pending/success/failure/cancellation plus result/error/progress.\n\n**Declared effects**: what categories of mutation/external action the Method is permitted or expected to perform.\n\nThen I think you've got something *very* strong.\n\nAnd I would **still leave \"what actually executes the method?\" unanswered**, exactly as you've done. Your graph explicitly says the execution mechanism remains unspecified.  That's not a hole yet. That's good restraint.\n\nBecause you're defining **the contract for callable behavior**, not accidentally defining `function foo() {}` with extra furniture.\n\nThe really tasty consequence is starting to appear now:\n\n**Properties describe readable/writable state.\nMethods describe callable capability.\nEvents describe observable occurrence.**\n\nAnd each has a crucial separation between **request and outcome**.\n\nThat's beginning to look less like \"scripting features\" and more like an actual **object/capability protocol for the graph**. ⚡\n"
        },
        "interfaceContract": {
          "version": 1,
          "receivesViewContent": true
        },
        "memo": "",
        "link": "",
        "identity": {
          "graphId": "twilite-scripting-methods-declaration"
        },
